{
  "_comment": [
    "A 16-node / 21-edge graph for unit-testing the router. NOT a sample of the real world.",
    "",
    "Node ids are GENERIC on purpose, exactly as routes.json is (CLAUDE.md 11): the shipped map",
    "uses real geography, but a FIXTURE must not encode behaviour against a named place. There is",
    "nowhere here to put a nationality, and that is the point.",
    "",
    "It is built so that every mechanism the five cost functions rely on has something to bite on:",
    "  - a tolled motorway corridor       start -> a1 -> a2 -> end     (fast, tolled x3)",
    "  - a scenic mountain corridor       start -> b1 -> b2 -> end     (scenic 3, terrainDifficulty 3)",
    "  - a controlled crossing            start -> c1 -> cross -> c2 -> end",
    "  - an UNCONTROLLED boundary edge    c1 -> c2                     (adminBoundary, no crossing node)",
    "    which only `illicit` may traverse, and which is the shortest route on distance",
    "  - a domestic ferry                 start -> p1 =[ferry]= p2 -> end",
    "  - a rail-only corridor             start -> m1 =[train]= end    (illicit masks train)",
    "  - a long seasonal desert corridor  start -> d1 -> d2 -> end     (winter_closed in the middle)",
    "  - a degree-1 spur                  end -> z1                    (for orphan/bridge tests)",
    "",
    "`modes` and `services` are written as arrays and converted to masks by the test loader, so",
    "the fixture stays readable and the mask helpers get exercised on the way in.",
    "",
    "distanceKm values are round numbers chosen to make several paths land close together, because",
    "near-ties are where tie-break nondeterminism hides (ADR 0025 Decision 3)."
  ],
